= Ginseng, Poria and Atractylodes Powder (Shēn Líng Bái Zhú Sǎn, 參苓白朮散)

_The great formula for *chronic* diarrhoea of Spleen vacuity with damp, and the most elaborated member of the Four Gentlemen family: ten ingredients, from the Song imperial *太平惠民和劑局方*. It is a derivative, so this entry does not restate the base architecture — see four-gentlemen-decoction.typ for 四君子湯 and the 補中有瀉 principle it embodies. What is distinctive here is everything added on top: *山藥* and *蓮子* to tonify and astringe, *白扁豆*, *薏苡仁* and *砂仁* to resolve damp by three different mechanisms, and — the formula's signature and most-discussed feature — *桔梗*, a Lung drug in a diarrhoea formula, included on the principle *培土生金* ("bank up Earth to generate Metal") and as a *舟楫之劑*, the "boat and oars" that carry the other drugs upward. Without it the formula would be relentlessly descending, which in a patient whose qi is already sinking would be the wrong direction. A note on the name: the user's *Líng Bái Zhú Sǎn* is read here as 參苓白朮散 with the leading 參 dropped, that being the only standard formula matching the string._

== Name & Meaning

*參苓白朮散* — the name is a plain *ingredient list*, not a metaphor: *參* (ginseng) + *苓* (poria) + *白朮* (atractylodes) + *散* (powder). Three of the ten drugs, named in order of weight, with the dosage form appended. *Contrast 四君子湯, whose name is a Confucian conceit, and 逍遙散, whose name is a Zhuangzi allusion* — this formula's name makes no claim and tells you what is in the packet. Chinese formula names run the full range from literary allusion to bare inventory, and this is the inventory end.

*Standard English renderings:* "Ginseng, Poria and Atractylodes Powder" (the form used elsewhere in this book, see `substances/cara.typ`); "Ginseng, Poria, and White Atractylodes Powder"; "Ginseng, Poria and Atractylodes Macrocephala Powder". Patent-medicine trade: *參苓白朮丸 / Shen Ling Bai Zhu Wan*. *Japanese (Kampo):* 参苓白朮散 *Jinryōbyakujutsusan*. *Korean:* 삼령백출산 *samnyeongbaekchulsan*.

*A disambiguation on the abbreviated name:* *苓* alone is ambiguous in formula names — it abbreviates *茯苓* here, but *豬苓* elsewhere (as in 豬苓湯), and *苓* appears in 苓桂朮甘湯 and 五苓散 meaning poria again. *白朮* is likewise abbreviated *朮*, which also stands for *蒼朮* in other names. Formula-name abbreviations are not reliable identifiers; the full character string is.

== Source & History

*太平惠民和劑局方 (Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng)*, the Song imperial formulary, 1107–1110 — *the same source as 四君子湯 and 四物湯*, both already in this book, and the text that standardized much of the tonifying formulary for the subsequent millennium.

*Its position in the family.* The Four Gentlemen lineage grows by addition, each step answering a problem the previous one leaves open: *四君子湯* tonifies Spleen qi; *異功散* adds 陳皮 so the supplementation does not stagnate; *六君子湯* adds 半夏 for the phlegm that Spleen vacuity generates; *香砂六君子湯* adds aromatics for frank pain and distension. *參苓白朮散 branches differently* — it is not the next link in that chain but a parallel elaboration for the case where *damp and chronic diarrhoea dominate the picture rather than accompanying it*, and where the Lung is drawn in. See `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ` for the chain.

*In modern practice* it is one of the most-used Chinese patent medicines, with a particularly heavy *paediatric* register — and the classical text itself anticipates this, closing its dose instruction with *"for children, adjust the dose according to age"*, which is an unusual thing for a Song formulary to spell out and a fair indication of who was taking it.#footnote[_Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (太平惠民和劑局方), 1107–1110 — the source text, consistent with this book's attribution for 四君子湯 and 四物湯. The paediatric dosing instruction (小兒量歲數加減服之) is in the formula's own text. Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed., for the formula's position in the Four Gentlemen family and modern standing.]

== Composition

*Classical proportions (Hejijufang), and they are not equal — the weighting is the formula's thesis.* Given in *jīn* (斤):
- *人參, 白朮, 白茯苓, 炙甘草, 山藥* — *2 jin each*
- *白扁豆* — *1.5 jin*
- *蓮子肉, 薏苡仁, 縮砂仁, 桔梗* — *1 jin each*

*The four Gentlemen plus 山藥 are double-weighted; the damp-resolving and aromatic additions are single.* This is a *tonic formula with a damp package*, not a damp formula with tonic support — and prescribing it as though the reverse were true is the commonest way to misuse it.

*Processing is specified per ingredient in the source*, and is part of the composition: *白扁豆* soaked in ginger juice, peeled and lightly fried; *桔梗* fried to a deep yellow; *甘草* fried (炙). These are not optional refinements — see Preparation.

*Modern clinical decoction doses* (reference figures requiring verification against current pharmacopoeial and clinical practice): 人參 9–12 g (or 黨參 12–15 g); 白朮 9–12 g; 茯苓 12–15 g; 山藥 12–15 g; 蓮子肉 9–12 g; 白扁豆 9–12 g; 薏苡仁 9–15 g; 砂仁 3–6 g (*後下*); 桔梗 6–9 g; 炙甘草 6 g.

*On the role assignments below:* the four Gentlemen core is treated as a single sovereign block rather than split, because its internal architecture is the subject of its own entry and restating it here would duplicate. Every ingredient still carries its own property line, per this book's self-contained rule.

=== 人參, 白朮, 茯苓 (君 — the sovereign triad, from Four Gentlemen)

- *人參 (rénshēn,* ginseng): sweet, slightly bitter; slightly warm (微溫); Spleen, Lung, Heart, Kidney. *大補元氣*, *補脾益肺*, *生津*, *安神益智*. See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`.
- *白朮 (báizhú,* atractylodes rhizome): bitter, sweet; warm (溫); Spleen, Stomach. *健脾益氣*, *燥濕利水*, *止汗*, *安胎*. Usually *炒白朮*. *No substance entry yet.*
- *茯苓 (fúlíng,* poria): sweet, bland (淡); neutral (平); Spleen, Heart, Kidney. *利水滲濕*, *健脾*, *寧心安神*. See `substances/fuling.typ`.

*Function in this formula:* the same tonify-and-drain core as the parent formula — 人參 supplies the qi, 白朮 strengthens the Spleen while *drying* damp, 茯苓 *leaches* it out — but here it is the *foundation* rather than the whole prescription, and the drying and leaching actions it already contains are reinforced by three further damp strategies below. *Note that the formula therefore attacks damp by four distinct mechanisms*, which is what the "damp predominant" indication requires.

=== 山藥 (shānyào, Chinese yam) — 臣, minister

*Properties:* Sweet (甘); neutral (平). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Lung (肺), Kidney (腎). *Principal actions:* *益氣養陰* (augments qi and nourishes yin), *補脾肺腎* (supplements Spleen, Lung and Kidney), *固精止帶*. A *Shennong Bencao Jing* 上品 drug with notably few classical contraindications.

*Function in this formula:* it is the *second pillar*, double-weighted alongside the Gentlemen, and it does something none of them does — it tonifies *qi and yin together*, and across *three* organs. Its *Lung* channel entry is what makes the formula's upper-body reach anatomically coherent rather than an afterthought of 桔梗 alone; its *Kidney* entry anchors the treatment in chronic, long-standing depletion. Being sweet-neutral and gently moistening, it also *offsets the drying* of 白朮 and the aromatics, so the formula can be given over months without desiccating the patient.

See `substances/cara.typ`, which treats the *Dioscorea* genus and gives 山藥 (*_D. polystachya_*) its full classical treatment.

=== 蓮子肉 (liánzǐròu, lotus seed) — 臣, minister

*Properties:* Sweet (甘), astringent (澀); neutral (平). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Kidney (腎), Heart (心). *Principal actions:* *補脾止瀉* (supplements the Spleen and checks diarrhoea), *益腎固精*, *養心安神*.

*Function in this formula:* it supplies the one action the Four Gentlemen core entirely lacks — *astringency*. The base formula drains and tonifies but does not *hold*; in chronic diarrhoea, holding is precisely what is wanted. 蓮子's *澀* flavour binds the intestines while its sweetness continues to supplement, so it checks the stool without opposing the tonification. Its Heart channel entry also addresses the disturbed sleep and timorousness that commonly accompany long depletion.

*No substance entry yet.*

=== 白扁豆 (báibiǎndòu, white hyacinth bean) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Sweet (甘); slightly warm (微溫) to neutral. *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃). *Principal actions:* *健脾化濕* (strengthens the Spleen and transforms damp), *和中消暑* (harmonizes the middle, resolves summerheat).

*Function in this formula:* a *gentle* damp-transformer that is simultaneously a food — it tonifies the Spleen without the heaviness of a frank tonic and without the harshness of a drying drug, which is exactly what a depleted, long-diarrhoeal patient can tolerate. Classically prepared here *soaked in ginger juice, peeled and lightly fried*, which warms it and removes the skin's harder, more difficult fraction.

*A safety note that belongs with the ingredient:* raw or undercooked hyacinth bean contains *lectins and cyanogenic glycosides* and can cause vomiting and gastroenteritis; thorough cooking destroys them, and the classical frying instruction performs that function. *Modern relevance is to the bean as food, not to the decocted drug*, but it is worth stating.

*No substance entry yet.*

=== 薏苡仁 (yìyǐrén, Job's tears) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Sweet (甘), bland (淡); cool (涼) to slightly cold (微寒). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺). *Principal actions:* *利水滲濕*, *健脾*, *除痺*, *清熱排膿*.

*Function in this formula:* it *leaches* damp downward and out through urination — the *淡味滲泄* mechanism it shares with 茯苓 — and its *Lung* channel entry reinforces the formula's upper reach. The *stir-fried* form (炒薏苡仁) is appropriate here, since the Spleen-strengthening and stool-binding emphasis is wanted rather than the raw drug's stronger cold draining.

See `substances/coix.typ` — *and note the pregnancy caution recorded there*, which this formula inherits and which is restated under Cautions.

=== 砂仁 (shārén, amomum fruit) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Acrid/pungent (辛); warm (溫); aromatic (芳香). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃). *Principal actions:* *化濕行氣* (transforms damp and moves qi), *溫中止瀉* (warms the middle and checks diarrhoea), *安胎*.

*Function in this formula — and it introduces a third damp mechanism.* The formula already *dries* damp (白朮) and *leaches* it (茯苓, 薏苡仁); 砂仁 *aromatically transforms* it — *芳香化濕*, the mechanism by which volatile aromatics rouse a damp-encumbered Spleen rather than attacking the damp directly. It also *moves qi*, which keeps a formula of eight sweet and bland drugs from becoming cloying and obstructive, and it *warms*, offsetting the coolness of 薏苡仁.

*Its volatility is a preparation constraint, not a detail*: the active fraction is the essential oil, and prolonged boiling drives it off, so 砂仁 is added *後下* (late). See Preparation.

*No substance entry yet.*

=== 桔梗 (jiégěng, platycodon root) — 佐使, assistant and envoy

*Properties:* Bitter (苦), acrid (辛); neutral (平). *Channels:* Lung (肺). *Principal actions:* *宣肺祛痰* (diffuses the Lung and expels phlegm), *利咽* (benefits the throat), *排膿* (expels pus), and — the action that matters here — *載藥上行* (carries the other medicines upward).

*Function in this formula — the signature design feature, and the one worth understanding properly.* A Lung drug in a chronic-diarrhoea formula looks like a mistake. Three reasons are given, and they are complementary rather than competing:
- *培土生金 (péi tǔ shēng jīn,* "bank up Earth to generate Metal") — the *五行* generation relation: the Spleen (Earth) *generates* the Lung (Metal), so chronic Spleen vacuity produces Lung symptoms — weak voice, shortness of breath, cough with thin white sputum, susceptibility to colds — and the correct treatment is *to strengthen the Spleen, not to treat the Lung directly*. 桔梗 is how the formula reaches the Lung it is already treating by proxy.
- *舟楫之劑 (zhōují zhī jì,* "the boat-and-oar agent") — the classical metaphor for a drug that *floats* a formula upward against its natural tendency. 桔梗 lifts the whole prescription.
- *Directional correction* — and this is the most practical reason. Every other active direction in this formula is *downward*: 茯苓 and 薏苡仁 drain through urination, 蓮子 and 白扁豆 bind the stool. In a patient with chronic diarrhoea, whose qi is *already sinking*, a purely descending formula would deepen the problem. 桔梗 supplies the countervailing lift. *Remove it and the formula is not merely less effective but pointed the wrong way.*

Classically fried to a *deep yellow*, which moderates its dispersing edge. *No substance entry yet.*

=== 炙甘草 (zhì gāncǎo, honey-fried licorice) — 使, envoy

*Properties:* Sweet (甘); *warm* (溫) in the honey-fried form — as against raw 生甘草, which is cool to neutral and clears heat. *Channels:* Heart (心), Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), and classically all twelve. *Principal actions:* *補脾益氣*, *調和諸藥* (harmonizes all the medicines), *緩急止痛*.

*Function in this formula:* supplements the Spleen alongside the sovereign, and *harmonizes* ten drugs into one prescription — a larger harmonizing job than in the four-ingredient parent. *The processing is required*: a formula built to warm and supplement the middle needs the honey-fried form, and substituting raw licorice would introduce a cooling, heat-clearing drug into a prescription designed to do the opposite.

See `substances/chinese-licorice.typ` and `substances/licorice-root.typ` — including the glycyrrhizin ceiling that governs this formula's safe duration.

== Formula Architecture

_For the tonify-and-drain core, see `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`; it is not restated here. What follows is what the six additions accomplish._

The pattern is *脾虛濕盛* — Spleen vacuity with *exuberant* damp — and its defining feature is *chronicity*. The Spleen's 運化 (transportation and transformation) has failed long enough that three things are simultaneously true: qi is depleted, damp has accumulated and will not clear, and the stool will not form. A formula that only tonified would feed the damp; one that only drained would deepen the depletion; one that only astringed would trap the damp. *參苓白朮散 does all three at once and in proportion*, which is why it has ten ingredients and why they are weighted as they are.

*Five actions, deliberately layered:*
- *Tonify* — 人參, 白朮, 茯苓, 山藥, 炙甘草 (double-weighted; the formula's centre of gravity).
- *Dry* damp — 白朮.
- *Leach* damp downward — 茯苓, 薏苡仁 (the *淡味滲泄* mechanism).
- *Aromatically transform* damp — 砂仁, 白扁豆 (*芳香化濕*).
- *Astringe* — 蓮子, 白扁豆.
- *and Lift* — 桔梗, against the four preceding downward actions.

*That damp is attacked by three different mechanisms is the formula's real sophistication.* Drying, leaching and aromatic transformation are not redundant: drying suits damp held in the tissues, leaching suits damp that can be routed to the bladder, and aromatic transformation suits a Spleen too torpid to move anything at all. A chronic case usually has all three.

*The 培土生金 axis is the second.* By treating the Lung through the Spleen rather than directly, the formula addresses a cluster — chronic loose stools *together with* a weak voice, breathlessness and recurrent colds — that looks like two unrelated complaints and is, in this system, one.

*What breaks if an ingredient is removed:*
- *Remove 桔梗* and the formula loses its only upward vector and its Lung reach; it becomes a competent diarrhoea formula and stops being this one.
- *Remove 蓮子 and 白扁豆* and nothing holds the stool; the drainers alone will not form it.
- *Remove 山藥* and the formula dries the patient over a long course, and loses the yin and Kidney dimension that chronicity requires.
- *Remove 砂仁* and ten sweet-bland drugs sit heavily on a Spleen that cannot move them.#footnote[Pattern mechanism, the 培土生金 and 舟楫之劑 readings of 桔梗, and the layered damp strategies: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed., and standard modern formula-theory teaching texts. The "remove an ingredient" analysis is the conventional pedagogical treatment. The three-mechanism framing of the damp-resolving drugs is this book's own organization of standard material.]

== Indications & Pattern

*Pattern:* *脾虛濕盛 / 脾胃虛弱夾濕* — Spleen-Stomach vacuity with damp encumbrance, *chronic*.

*Presenting signs:*
- *大便溏瀉 / 久瀉* — loose, unformed stools or long-standing diarrhoea, often with undigested food; *not* foul-smelling, *not* urgent, *not* burning.
- *飲食不化, 食少* — food not transformed; poor appetite.
- *胸脘痞悶* — oppression and stuffiness of the chest and epigastrium.
- *四肢乏力, 形體消瘦* — weak limbs, wasting; in children, failure to thrive.
- *面色萎黃* — a sallow-yellow complexion.
- *氣短咳嗽* — shortness of breath, and cough with thin white sputum (the Lung component).
- *Tongue:* pale (舌淡), with a *white greasy coat* (苔白膩). *Pulse:* *虛緩* — weak and moderate-to-slow, or soft (濡).

*The diagnostic triad is vacuity signs + white greasy coat + chronic unformed stool.* All three. A greasy coat without vacuity is a different formula; vacuity without the coat is 四君子湯.

*Modern correlates — correlations, not equivalences:* chronic diarrhoea and IBS-D presentations, malabsorption states, chronic colitis in remission, paediatric chronic diarrhoea and poor growth, convalescence after prolonged illness, and chemotherapy-associated gastrointestinal upset. *These are contexts in which the pattern is often found, not diagnoses the formula treats* — and see the modern trap below.

*Differentiation from adjacent formulas:*
- *四君子湯* — vacuity *without* significant damp or diarrhoea. See `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *六君子湯* — vacuity with phlegm-damp presenting *upward*: nausea, vomiting, copious sputum. This formula presents *downward*.
- *香砂六君子湯* — vacuity with frank epigastric *pain and distension*.
- *補中益氣湯* — vacuity with *sinking*: prolapse, bearing-down, vacuity fever. Shares the lifting idea but makes it the central action.
- *理中丸* — frank Spleen-Stomach *cold*: cold limbs, watery diarrhoea, pain relieved by warmth.
- *痛瀉要方* — Liver-Spleen disharmony: pain *preceding* the stool with relief after, stress-linked, wiry pulse.
- *四神丸* — Kidney-yang vacuity *五更瀉* (daybreak diarrhoea), with cold limbs and lumbar soreness.
- *平胃散* — damp encumbrance *without* vacuity: a robust patient with a thick greasy coat.

== Misapplication & Look-Alike Patterns

_This formula's characteristic error is specific and worth naming at the outset: *it astringes*. Giving an astringing formula to a diarrhoea caused by something the body is trying to expel — heat, damp-heat, food stagnation, infection — does not merely fail; it traps the pathogen. That is the mechanism behind most of what follows. Consequences below are clinical commonplaces except where flagged._

=== If given to the wrong pattern

- *Damp-heat diarrhoea (濕熱瀉) — the dangerous error.* *Mechanism:* the formula is sweet, warm and *astringent*; sweetness and warmth feed damp-heat, and the binding action closes the exit on a pathogen the body is clearing. This is *閉門留寇* ("shutting the door and leaving the bandit inside") in its most literal application. *Consequence:* worsened urgency and burning, increasingly foul stool, abdominal pain, a yellowing greasy coat, sometimes fever — and the deterioration can be brisk. *Correction:* clear the damp-heat first (葛根芩連湯, 白頭翁湯 register); astringe only after.
- *Acute diarrhoea, dysentery, or food poisoning.* *Mechanism:* the same — astringing an acute excess. *Consequence:* a protracted illness and retained pathogen. *Correction:* never astringe before clearing; the classical rule is firm and the modern one agrees.
- *Food stagnation diarrhoea.* *Mechanism:* ten mostly sweet and filling drugs added to a middle already blocked by undigested food. *Consequence:* worsened distension, sour belching, aversion to food. *Correction:* disperse first (保和丸), then tonify if vacuity remains.
- *Stomach or Spleen yin vacuity.* *Mechanism:* 白朮, 砂仁 and the aromatics are drying; the patient's deficit is fluid. *Consequence:* worsened dry mouth, dry retching, a redder and barer tongue. *Correction:* a moistening formula; 山藥 alone will not carry a dry patient through this prescription.
- *Kidney-yang vacuity diarrhoea (五更瀉).* *Mechanism:* the formula has no warming Kidney drug; the deficit is below what it reaches. *Consequence:* not harm but *under-treatment* — weeks of partial benefit while the root is untouched. *Correction:* 四神丸, or the two together where both patterns coexist.
- *Acute exterior illness.* The standing rule against tonifying an unresolved exterior; release first.

=== Patterns mistaken for this one

- *Damp-heat diarrhoea mistaken for Spleen-vacuity diarrhoea — the critical confusion, and the one that causes harm.* *Why it resembles:* both present as chronic loose stools with a greasy tongue coat and poor appetite, and a damp-heat patient who has been ill for months looks depleted. *Distinguishing signs:* the coat is *yellow* greasy, not white; the stool is *foul-smelling*, with *urgency* and *burning*; the pulse is *slippery and rapid* (滑數), not weak and moderate. *The coat colour alone will usually decide it.* *What to give instead:* 葛根芩連湯 or the 白頭翁湯 register; return to this formula only once the heat has cleared and vacuity remains.
- *Liver-Spleen disharmony (痛瀉).* *Why it resembles:* chronic loose stools with poor appetite and fatigue. *Distinguishing sign:* the *sequence* — pain builds, the stool follows, the pain is *relieved* by it — together with a *wiry* pulse and symptoms that track stress. *What to give instead:* 痛瀉要方, or 逍遙散 where constraint dominates. (This is the mirror of the confusion recorded in `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`, where Liver constraint masquerades as plain Spleen vacuity.)
- *Kidney-yang vacuity (五更瀉).* *Why it resembles:* chronic diarrhoea with exhaustion. *Distinguishing sign:* the *timing* — diarrhoea *before dawn*, waking the patient — with cold limbs and lumbar soreness. *What to give instead:* 四神丸.
- *Food stagnation.* *Distinguishing sign:* *aversion* to food rather than reduced appetite, sour or rotten belching, discomfort *worse* with pressure, thick coat. *What to give instead:* 保和丸.
- *The modern trap — and for this formula it is a safety matter, not just a classification one.* Chronic diarrhoea is a *presenting complaint*, not a diagnosis, and it maps onto biomedical conditions that a pattern-matched herbal formula can *mask*: *coeliac disease* and *lactose intolerance* both produce exactly this picture and are both testable; *inflammatory bowel disease*, *chronic infection* (including giardiasis), *bile-acid malabsorption*, *pancreatic insufficiency* and *colorectal malignancy* all can. *A formula that improves the symptom while an undiagnosed enteropathy continues is doing harm by concealment.* The red flags that take precedence over any pattern diagnosis are *blood in the stool, unintended weight loss, nocturnal diarrhoea, fever, anaemia, onset after age 50, and a family history of IBD or bowel cancer* — any of which warrants biomedical workup first. *This is the point at which pattern reasoning should defer rather than compete*, and it is stated here because 參苓白朮散 is precisely the formula a chronic-diarrhoea patient is likely to be offered.#footnote[The astringing-an-excess mechanism and 閉門留寇 applied to diarrhoea: standard formula-theory literature; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. Differentiation of 脾虛瀉 from 濕熱瀉, 痛瀉, and 五更瀉 by coat colour, symptom sequence and timing is conventional clinical teaching. The red-flag list for chronic diarrhoea follows standard gastroenterology guidance and is this book's own framing of where pattern reasoning should defer to biomedical workup.]

== Modifications & Derived Formulas

*Common modifications (加減):*
- *+ 陳皮* — for more marked qi stagnation and fullness; this variant is widely used and is the version `substances/tangerine-peel.typ` records.
- *+ 乾薑, 附子* — for a cold component.
- *+ 黃連* — where mild heat complicates the damp (used cautiously; heat proper contraindicates the base formula).
- *+ 木香* — for pain and distension, toward the 香砂 register.
- *+ 訶子, 肉豆蔻* — for more obstinate chronic diarrhoea needing stronger astringency.
- *黨參 for 人參* — the routine modern substitution, milder and cheaper at larger dose.
- *Omit 桔梗* — occasionally done where there is no Lung involvement, and worth flagging as a *substantive* change rather than a simplification: it removes the formula's only lifting action.

*Related formulas rather than derivatives:* the Four Gentlemen family (`formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`) — *異功散*, *六君子湯*, *香砂六君子湯* — are siblings reached by a different route. *七味白朮散* (with 藿香, 葛根) is the closer paediatric relative for diarrhoea with thirst.

== Preparation & Administration

*Classical method (Hejijufang).* The ten drugs ground to a *fine powder* (為細末); *2 錢 (qián)* per dose — on the order of *7–8 g* at roughly 3.7 g to the qian — *taken down with 棗湯, jujube decoction*. And *"for children, adjust the dose according to age."*

*The vehicle is part of the prescription.* The powder is not taken with water but with a decoction of *大棗* (jujube) — itself a sweet, warm, Spleen-supplementing drug (see `substances/jujube-dates.typ`) — so the carrier reinforces the formula's purpose. *This is lost in every modern form*, which takes the powder or pills with plain water, and it is a genuine if minor departure from the original.

*Modern decoction:*
1. Combine at the modern doses above, *omitting 砂仁*.
2. Soak in cold water 20–30 minutes; bring to a boil, then simmer; two successive decoctions, liquids combined.
3. *Add 砂仁 in the last 5 minutes (後下)* — its action resides in a volatile oil that prolonged boiling drives off. *This is the single preparation detail most often got wrong*, and a formula boiled with the amomum from the start has lost one of its three damp mechanisms.
4. Divide into two doses daily, taken *warm*.

*Processing requirements, from the source:* 白扁豆 ginger-juice-soaked, peeled and lightly fried; 桔梗 fried to deep yellow; 甘草 honey-fried. Modern dispensing usually supplies these forms, but they should be specified rather than assumed.

*Timing and course.* Taken *before meals* or between them, as a tonic. *The course is long* — this is a chronic-condition formula, reckoned in weeks to months, and it is gentle enough for that: it contains no 半夏, no aconite, and nothing requiring the dose vigilance that the harsher formulas demand. The licorice is the limiting factor (below).

*Modern prepared forms.* *參苓白朮丸* and granules are among the most widely sold Chinese patents, including paediatric presentations. Concentration ratios vary by manufacturer and are *not interchangeable gram-for-gram* with raw-herb decoction; and pill forms cannot reproduce the *後下* handling of 砂仁, which is a real if unquantified difference.#footnote[Classical preparation, the 棗湯 vehicle, and the paediatric dosing instruction are in the formula's text in the _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_; the wording is paraphrased here as to its shape and flagged for verification against a specific edition. The 錢 conversion (~3.7 g, with period and regional variation) follows this book's usage elsewhere. 砂仁 as 後下 and the volatile-oil rationale: standard Chinese pharmacy practice. Granule-to-decoction non-equivalence: manufacturer labelling.]

== Cautions & Contraindications

_Pattern-matching errors and their consequences are treated above under Misapplication. This section covers pharmacological safety._

*Pattern contraindications (summary; mechanisms under Misapplication):* damp-heat and heat-pattern diarrhoea; acute diarrhoea, dysentery and food poisoning; food stagnation; yin-vacuity dryness; acute exterior illness.

*Ingredient-derived cautions:*
- *炙甘草 / glycyrrhizin — the limiting factor on duration.* Sustained licorice intake produces a *pseudoaldosteronism* picture: sodium and water retention, *hypokalaemia*, oedema and *raised blood pressure*. Since this formula is characteristically taken for *months*, the licorice ceiling matters more here than in a short-course prescription. *Caution or avoid* in hypertension, oedema, hypokalaemia, heart failure and renal impairment; *interactions* with diuretics, corticosteroids and digoxin. See `substances/licorice-root.typ`.
- *人參* — the ginseng interaction profile applies: reduced INR with *warfarin*; additive hypoglycaemia with *antidiabetic medication*; case reports with *MAO inhibitors*; caution in uncontrolled hypertension. Classically *人參反藜蘆* (十八反). See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`.
- *薏苡仁 — an inherited pregnancy caution.* `substances/coix.typ` records the classical *妊娠慎用* for Job's tears, supported by modern reports of uterine-stimulant activity. *This formula inherits it*, which is worth stating because 參苓白朮散 is otherwise gentle enough to seem unproblematic in pregnancy. Practitioner direction, not self-prescription.
- *白扁豆* — raw or undercooked hyacinth bean contains lectins and cyanogenic glycosides; the classical frying and ordinary decoction both address this, but the caution belongs on record.
- *砂仁* — no significant interaction profile; the practical issue is preparation, not safety.

*Paediatric use.* The formula is classically and currently given to children, with the source text itself directing age-adjusted dosing. *That it is traditional does not make it self-prescribable*: paediatric chronic diarrhoea warrants a diagnosis before a prescription, for the reasons given under the modern trap above.

*Reference information, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Glycyrrhizin pseudoaldosteronism and its dose-duration relation, and the interactions with diuretics, corticosteroids and digoxin: see `substances/licorice-root.typ` and current herb-drug interaction literature; the point that duration matters more in a chronically-taken formula is this book's inference from the composition and the course. Ginseng interactions: `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`. The 薏苡仁 pregnancy caution: `substances/coix.typ`. Hyacinth-bean lectin/cyanogenic content: standard food-toxicology literature.]

== Modern Pharmacology

*The evidence situation first.* 參苓白朮散 has a *substantial* Chinese clinical literature by the standards of classical formulas — chronic diarrhoea, IBS-D, paediatric diarrhoea and anorexia, chronic gastritis, ulcerative colitis in remission, and chemotherapy-associated gastrointestinal toxicity — but it is *predominantly Chinese-language, small-trial, heterogeneous in design, and subject to the well-documented positive-result publication bias of that literature*. It should be read as suggestive rather than established.

- *Chronic diarrhoea and IBS* — the best-represented indication, with multiple small randomized trials and meta-analyses reporting symptomatic benefit, frequently as an adjunct. Methodological quality is the limiting factor in interpreting them.
- *Gut barrier and microbiota* — an active preclinical area: reported effects on intestinal mucosal barrier integrity, tight-junction proteins, short-chain fatty acid production and microbiota composition. *Mechanistically plausible and congruent with the classical 運化 framing*, but preclinical.
- *Immune and anti-inflammatory* — polysaccharide fractions from 山藥, 茯苓 and 薏苡仁 show immunomodulatory activity individually. *This is single-herb evidence and is not evidence for the compound* — a distinction this book insists on, and one that reviews of this formula frequently blur.
- *Paediatric malnutrition and appetite* — small Chinese trials; preliminary.

*What has not been shown:* that the ten-drug combination outperforms its components or a simpler Spleen tonic, which is the specific claim the architecture makes; and any robust non-Chinese replication. *Note also that the better-evidenced formula in this family is the sibling 六君子湯 (Kampo Rikkunshitō)*, with its ghrelin-signalling mechanism — see `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ` — and *that evidence does not transfer here*, the formulas differing in six of ten ingredients.#footnote[Chinese clinical literature on 參苓白朮散 in chronic diarrhoea, IBS, paediatric indications and chemotherapy-associated GI toxicity: predominantly Chinese-language randomized trials and meta-analyses of variable quality — verify against current systematic reviews, and note the documented positive-result publication bias in this literature. Gut-barrier and microbiota findings are preclinical. The single-herb polysaccharide literature is explicitly not adduced as evidence for the compound. Rikkunshitō evidence belongs to 六君子湯 and is not transferable.]

== Sources

- _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (太平惠民和劑局方), Song imperial formulary, 1107–1110 — the source text: composition, the unequal jin proportions, the per-ingredient processing instructions, the 棗湯 vehicle, and the paediatric dosing direction.
- Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press) — composition, architecture, the 培土生金 and 舟楫之劑 readings of 桔梗, differentiation, and modifications.
- Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. — the single-drug properties summarized here.
- _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ (current edition) — monographs for 人參, 白朮, 茯苓, 山藥, 蓮子, 白扁豆, 薏苡仁, 砂仁, 桔梗, 甘草 and their processed forms.
- Current Chinese clinical and preclinical literature on 參苓白朮散 (chronic diarrhoea, IBS, paediatric indications, gut barrier and microbiota) — verify against current systematic reviews.
- Substance entries for the ingredients: `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`, `substances/fuling.typ`, `substances/cara.typ` (山藥, within the _Dioscorea_ genus entry), `substances/coix.typ`, `substances/chinese-licorice.typ`, `substances/licorice-root.typ`, and `substances/jujube-dates.typ` for the 棗湯 vehicle. *No substance entry yet exists for 白朮, 蓮子, 白扁豆, 砂仁 or 桔梗*; their properties are summarized in full above and the terms are in `zz-glossary.typ`.
- Companion formula entries: `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ` (the base formula and family chain), `formulas/four-substances-decoction.typ`.
- Foundational frameworks: `climates.typ`, `tibb-al-arabi.typ`, `zz-glossary.typ`.
